  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Mexican Veracruz-Gulf-Coast Afro-Mestizo-Indigenous son tradition with characteristic jarana five-double-course strum-guitar, requinto-jarocho four-string lead-melody-guitar, harp, quijada-de-burro donkey-jawbone scraper, and zapateado wooden-platform dance-percussion. Originated late-17th-century in the Sotavento lowlands south of Veracruz-Port out of Afro-Cuban habanera, Spanish-baroque fandango, and Indigenous-Totonac musical-fusion. The canonical La Bamba is the most internationally recognized son jarocho son, but the tradition spans 80+ son sones with characteristic 6/8-against-2/4 sesquialteral hemiola, call-and-response sung verses (often improvised, decimas Spanish-poetic-form), and circular fandango community-dance-and-music-making fiesta-context. The 1970s-1980s son-jarocho-revival reconnected urban Mexico City to rural Sotavento source-communities.",
